block/file-win32: mark only CD-ROM host devices as variable-length

hdev_refresh_limits() sets has_variable_length for every Windows host device, so a
raw hard disk has its geometry queried from the device for every length lookup:
three IOCTL_DISK_GET_DRIVE_GEOMETRY_EX per read request with virtio-blk. Measured on
a USB SSD under Windows 11 (QEMU 11.1.0, WHPX and TCG alike): about 3.5 ms a request
with the host idle, against 0.28 ms for the read itself. file-posix sets the flag in
cdrom_refresh_limits() only.

Keephaven carries this until it is upstream.
---
--- a/block/file-win32.c	2026-10-06 23:11:21.627860614 -0400
+++ b/block/file-win32.c	2026-10-06 23:11:21.630695749 -0400
@@ -802,9 +802,19 @@
 
 static void hdev_refresh_limits(BlockDriverState *bs, Error **errp)
 {
+    BDRVRawState *s = bs->opaque;
+
     /* XXX Does Windows support AIO on less than 512-byte alignment? */
     bs->bl.request_alignment = 512;
-    bs->bl.has_variable_length = true;
+    /*
+     * Only removable media change size under a running guest. With this set,
+     * every length query goes to the device (raw_co_getlength issues
+     * IOCTL_DISK_GET_DRIVE_GEOMETRY_EX), and the block layer queries the
+     * length for every request: once in virtio-blk's range check and once per
+     * node in bdrv_aligned_preadv(). On a USB disk that is about 1 ms each.
+     * file-posix sets it for CD-ROMs only (cdrom_refresh_limits()).
+     */
+    bs->bl.has_variable_length = (s->type == FTYPE_CD);
 }
 
 static int hdev_open(BlockDriverState *bs, QDict *options, int flags,
